title: LSP and MCP

!! Two protocols, one wiki

`--mcp` and `--lsp` are separate commands that compose on one command line:

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp rw listen --lsp port=6009
```

Both run in the same process against the same wiki store. A tiddler an assistant creates over MCP is a title your editor completes moments later, and a tiddler it renames stops being offered. Two separate processes would each boot their own copy of the wiki and drift apart.

Neither command depends on the other: what they share is code. The editor's [[find references|LSP Find References]] and the [[find_calls]] MCP tool read call sites from the same library, and the editor's [[go to definition|LSP Go to Definition]] and the [[find_definition]] MCP tool find the running ~JavaScript through the same module. So each works alone, and together an assistant and your editor agree on every call and definition.

!!! Why they are not one command

The two protocols disagree below the wiki. MCP `2026-07-28` is stateless: every message carries its own version and identity, and the server keeps nothing per connection. LSP is stateful by construction: it negotiates capabilities once, mirrors every buffer the editor opens, and stops answering after `shutdown`.

Folding that lifecycle into `--mcp` would either erode its stateless contract, or grow a second stack beside it that shares nothing but the wiki.

!!! The one thing they cannot share

Framing. MCP over stdio separates messages with newlines, while LSP counts bytes in a `Content-Length` header. One stdin cannot carry both, so `--lsp stdio` and `--mcp` refuse to start together, whichever you list first:

```
Error: --lsp stdio cannot run beside --mcp: both would read the same pipe, and their framings differ. Use --lsp port=<n> instead.
```

Give the LSP server a port and the two coexist.
